=== B.2 CF Conventions
<b.2-cf-conventions>
The Climate and Forecast (CF) conventions are the standard for climate
and forecast data stored in NetCDF format.

- #strong[CF Conventions Homepage];: https:\/\/cfconventions.org/
- #strong[CF Metadata Conventions (PDF)];:
  https:\/\/cfconventions.org/Data/cf-conventions/cf-conventions-1.11/cf-conventions.html
- #strong[Standard Variable Names];:
  https:\/\/cfconventions.org/Data/cf-standard-names/current/build/cf-standard-name-table.html

`{note} Always verify that your NetCDF files follow CF conventions. CDO and most climate analysis tools rely on CF-compliant metadata for correct operation.`

=== B.3 Key CF Concepts
<b.3-key-cf-concepts>
- #strong[Coordinate variables];: Latitude (`lat`), longitude (`lon`),
  time (`time`), vertical (`lev`/`plev`)
- #strong[Standard names];: Standardized variable names like
  `air_temperature`, `precipitation_amount`
- #strong[Units];: CF-compliant units (K for temperature, kg m-2 s-1 for
  precipitation rate, etc.)
- #strong[Bounds];: Cell boundaries for grid cells and time intervals
- #strong[Cell methods];: Description of how data was processed (e.g.,
  `time: mean`)

```bash
# Check CF compliance of a file
cdo nmlist temperature.nc | findstr "standard_name"
cdo nmlist temperature.nc | findstr "units"
cdo nmlist temperature.nc | findstr "cell_methods"
```

== C. GRIB Documentation and WMO Standards
<c.-grib-documentation-and-wmo-standards>
=== C.1 GRIB Resources
<c.1-grib-resources>
- #strong[GRIB Specification (WMO)];: https:\/\/github.com/wmo-im/GRIB2
- #strong[ECMWF GRIB Guide];:
  https:\/\/confluence.ecmwf.int/display/UDOC/GRIB+data+files
- #strong[cfgrib (Python GRIB reader)];:
  https:\/\/github.com/ecmwf/cfgrib

=== C.2 Working with GRIB in CDO
<c.2-working-with-grib-in-cdo>
```bash
# Get GRIB information
cdo sinfo grib_file.grib

# Convert GRIB to NetCDF
cdo -f nc copy grib_file.grib netcdf_file.nc

# Select GRIB parameters by shortName
cdo selname,t grib_file.grib temperature.grib

# Select by GRIB level
cdo sellev,500 pressure_level.grib
```

#raw("{warning} GRIB files can contain multiple parameters, levels, and time steps in a single file. Always use `cdo sinfo` to understand the file structure before processing.")

=== C.3 Key GRIB Concepts
<c.3-key-grib-concepts>
- #strong[GRIB Edition];: GRIB1 (older, limited), GRIB2 (current
  standard)
- #strong[Parameter];: Variable identified by parameter number (e.g.,
  130 = temperature in GRIB1)
- #strong[Level type];: Pressure level, surface, height above ground,
  etc.
- #strong[Data representation];: Grid type (regular lat-lon, Gaussian,
  reduced Gaussian)
- #strong[Bits per value];: Precision of packed data (more bits = higher
  precision)

