== G. Glossary of Terms
<g.-glossary-of-terms>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 66.67%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Term], [Definition],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Anomaly];], [Deviation of a value from its climatological
    mean],
    [#strong[CF Conventions];], [Climate and Forecast metadata
    conventions for NetCDF],
    [#strong[Climatology];], [Long-term average of a climate variable
    (usually 30 years)],
    [#strong[CMIP];], [Coupled Model Intercomparison Project],
    [#strong[Ensemble];], [Collection of model runs with different
    initial conditions or physics],
    [#strong[ESGF];], [Earth System Grid Federation - CMIP data
    distribution],
    [#strong[ETCCDI];], [Expert Team on Climate Change Detection and
    Indices],
    [#strong[GRIB];], [GRIdded Binary - WMO standard format for
    meteorological data],
    [#strong[NetCDF];], [Network Common Data Form - self-describing
    scientific data format],
    [#strong[Reanalysis];], [Gridded dataset combining observations with
    model forecasts via data assimilation],
    [#strong[Regridding];], [Interpolating data from one grid to
    another],
    [#strong[Remapping];], [Same as regridding (CDO terminology)],
    [#strong[SPI];], [Standardized Precipitation Index],
    [#strong[Trend];], [Long-term change in a variable, usually computed
    by linear regression],
    [#strong[Unstructured Grid];], [Grid without regular spatial
    structure (e.g., triangular mesh)],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

== H. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
<h.-frequently-asked-questions-faq>
=== H.1 General CDO Questions
<h.1-general-cdo-questions>
#strong[Q: CDO says "Error: Unsupported grid type" - what does this
mean?] A: Your file may use a grid type that CDO does not support for
the requested operation. Try `cdo griddes` to see the grid type. For
reduced Gaussian or unstructured grids, remap to a regular grid first.

#strong[Q: How do I process very large files without running out of
memory?] A: Use the `-L` flag for large-file mode, process data in
chunks by year or variable, and use piped operations to avoid
intermediate files.

#strong[Q: What is the difference between `cdo mean` and `cdo timmean`?]
A: `mean` averages over ALL dimensions. `timmean` averages only over the
time dimension, preserving spatial structure.

#strong[Q: How do I select a specific variable from a multi-variable
file?] A: Use `cdo selname,varname input.nc output.nc` or
`cdo selvar,varname input.nc output.nc`.

#strong[Q: CDO is slow on my Windows machine. What can I do?] A: Use the
`-P N` flag for parallel processing. Install via conda-forge (better
optimization). Use pipes. Consider Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).

=== H.2 Data-Specific Questions
<h.2-data-specific-questions>
#strong[Q: My NetCDF file has longitude values from 0 to 360. How do I
convert to -180 to 180?] A: Use
`cdo sellonlatbox,-180,180,-90,90 input.nc output.nc` or
`cdo remapbil,r360x180 input.nc output.nc`.

#strong[Q: How do I handle missing values in CDO?] A: CDO automatically
recognizes missing values from the NetCDF `_FillValue` or missing\_value
attribute. You can set missing values with `cdo setmissval,new_value`.

#strong[Q: What do I do if my time axis is in "days since …" but I need
dates?] A: CDO automatically interprets time units. Use `cdo showdate`
to see calendar dates, `cdo showtime` for times.

#strong[Q: My GRIB file won't load in CDO. What's wrong?] A: Check if
ecCodes is installed (CDO needs it for GRIB2). Try `cdo -v` to verify
GRIB support. Convert to NetCDF first.

=== H.3 Error Troubleshooting
<h.3-error-troubleshooting>
#strong[Q: "Segmentation fault" when running CDO - what now?] A: This
usually indicates a memory issue. Try: reduce parallel threads, add `-L`
flag, process smaller chunks, update CDO to latest version.

#strong[Q: "Error: Too many open files"] A: Increase your system's open
file limit. On Linux: `ulimit -n 4096`. In pipes, ensure each operator
completes before the next starts.

#strong[Q: "Error: cdo remapbil: Input file contains grid cell corners?
Use remapycon instead?"] A: This happens when the input has curvilinear
coordinates. Try `remapycon` (conservative remapping for curvilinear
grids) or preprocess with `cdo setgridtype,regular`.

== I. Best Practices Checklist
<i.-best-practices-checklist>
=== I.1 Data Management
<i.1-data-management>
- ☐ Use descriptive filenames (e.g., `era5_t2m_1980_2020_monthly.nc`)
- ☐ Keep raw data separate from processed data
- ☐ Document processing steps in scripts (not manual commands)
- ☐ Use version control (git) for analysis scripts
- ☐ Maintain a data inventory (what, where, when, how)
- ☐ Compress large files with `-z zip_5`
- ☐ Verify data integrity after download (checksums)

=== I.2 Workflow Practices
<i.2-workflow-practices>
- ☐ Always use `-O` flag in scripts (overwrite without prompting)
- ☐ Use `-P N` for CPU-intensive operations
- ☐ Pre-compute remapping weights when processing multiple files
- ☐ Chain operations with pipes to avoid intermediate files
- ☐ Log all processing steps with timestamps
- ☐ Use error handling (`set -euo pipefail` in bash)
- ☐ Test pipelines on small subsets before full data runs

=== I.3 Quality Assurance
<i.3-quality-assurance>
- ☐ Inspect data with `cdo info` before processing
- ☐ Check for missing values and outliers
- ☐ Verify time axis with `cdo showdate`
- ☐ Check grid description with `cdo griddes`
- ☐ Validate results against known values or benchmarks
- ☐ Document assumptions and thresholds used
- ☐ Cross-check with independent datasets

=== I.4 Reproducibility
<i.4-reproducibility>
- ☐ Save all processing commands in version-controlled scripts
- ☐ Record software versions (CDO, Python libraries)
- ☐ Document data sources with DOIs or access dates
- ☐ Use random seeds where applicable
- ☐ Package results with scripts and data citations
- ☐ Create README files explaining the workflow

