== L. Troubleshooting Guide
<l.-troubleshooting-guide>
=== L.1 Installation Problems
<l.1-installation-problems>
#strong[Problem: CDO not found after installation]

```bash
# Check if installed
cdo --version
# If not found, add to PATH
export PATH=/path/to/cdo/bin:$PATH   # Linux/macOS
# On Windows, ensure conda environment is activated
conda activate climate
```

#strong[Problem: GRIB support missing]

```bash
# Check GRIB support
cdo -v | findstr grib
# Install ecCodes alongside CDO
conda install -c conda-forge cdo eccodes
```

#strong[Problem: Compilation errors (building from source)] Check
dependencies: zlib, hdf5, netcdf-c, eccodes, proj, udunits.

```bash
sudo apt-get install libhdf5-dev libnetcdf-dev libeccodes-dev   # Ubuntu
brew install hdf5 netcdf eccodes                                  # macOS
```

=== L.2 Runtime Errors
<l.2-runtime-errors>
#strong[Problem: "Segmentation fault (core dumped)"] - Cause: Usually
memory exhaustion or incompatible file format - Solution: Use `-L` flag,
reduce threads with `-P 2`, process smaller chunks

#strong[Problem: "Unsupported grid type"] - Cause: Reduced Gaussian,
curvilinear, or unstructured grid - Solution: Convert to regular grid
first with `cdo remapbil,r360x180`

#strong[Problem: "Time axis not monotonic"] - Cause: File contains
unsorted or duplicate time steps - Solution: Use `cdo sorttimestamp` or
`cdo mergetime` to fix

#strong[Problem: "Operator not found"] - Cause: Typo or operator not
available in your CDO version - Solution: Check spelling, update CDO, or
use alternative operator

#strong[Problem: "Too many open files"] - Cause: System file descriptor
limit reached - Solution: Increase limit: `ulimit -n 10000`
(Linux/macOS)

=== L.3 Data Issues
<l.3-data-issues>
#strong[Problem: Missing values not recognized]

```bash
# Set missing value explicitly
cdo setmissval,-999 data.nc fixed.nc
```

#strong[Problem: Longitude range 0-360 vs -180 to 180 mismatch]

```bash
# Convert 0-360 to -180 to 180
cdo sellonlatbox,-180,180,-90,90 data_360.nc data_180.nc
```

#strong[Problem: Inconsistent time units between files]

```bash
# CDO may fail when merging files with different time units
# Use setreftime to standardize
cdo setreftime,1900-01-01,00:00:00,days file1.nc file1_fixed.nc
```

#strong[Problem: Variables renamed or missing expected names]

```bash
# Find available variables
cdo showname data.nc
# Rename if needed
cdo chname,old_var,new_var data.nc renamed.nc
```

=== L.4 Performance Troubleshooting
<l.4-performance-troubleshooting>
#strong[Issue: CDO is very slow] - Use `-P N` with appropriate N (number
of cores) - Avoid intermediate files -- use pipes - Check disk speed
(SSD recommended for large files) - Use `-f nc4 -z zip_1` for faster
compression - Precompute remapping weights

#strong[Issue: Out of memory] - Use `-L` flag for large-file mode -
Process in temporal chunks (e.g., year by year) - Select only needed
variables with `selname` - Use `-f nc4` which is more memory-efficient
than classic NetCDF

== M. Key CDO Commands Quick Reference
<m.-key-cdo-commands-quick-reference>
```bash
# File Information
cdo infov file.nc              # Variable info with statistics
cdo sinfo file.grib             # GRIB file structure
cdo griddes file.nc             # Grid description
cdo showdate file.nc            # Time steps as dates
cdo showyear file.nc            # Years present
cdo nmlist file.nc              # NetCDF metadata dump

# Selection
cdo selname,tas file.nc out.nc  # Select variable
cdo seldate,2000-01-01 file.nc  # Select by date
cdo sellonlatbox,lon1,lon2,lat1,lat2 file.nc  # Spatial crop
cdo seltimestep,1/10 file.nc    # Select timesteps 1-10

# Temporal
cdo daymean file.nc out.nc      # Daily mean
cdo monmean file.nc out.nc      # Monthly mean
cdo yearmean file.nc out.nc     # Annual mean
cdo ymonmean file.nc out.nc     # Monthly climatology
cdo ymonsub file.nc out.nc      # Monthly anomaly

# Spatial
cdo fldmean file.nc out.nc      # Spatial mean
cdo remapbil,target.nc src.nc out.nc  # Bilinear remap
cdo genbil,target.nc src.nc w.nc     # Generate weights

# Statistics
cdo timmean file.nc out.nc      # Temporal mean
cdo timpctl,90 file.nc min.nc max.nc out.nc  # 90th percentile
cdo ensmean f1.nc f2.nc f3.nc out.nc  # Ensemble mean

# Indices
cdo yearsum -ltc,0 tn.nc fd.nc  # Frost days
cdo yearmax -runsum,5 pr.nc rx5day.nc  # Max 5-day precip
```

#raw("{tip} Save this quick reference as a cheat sheet. The most commonly used CDO operators are: `selname`, `sellonlatbox`, `seldate`, `fldmean`, `timmean`, `monmean`, `yearmean`, `ymonsub`, `remapbil`, `ensmean`, and `output`.")

== N. Version Information
<n.-version-information>
```bash
# Check your CDO version
cdo --version

# Check installed components (NetCDF, GRIB, etc.)
cdo -v

# Check Python library versions
python -c "import xarray; print(xarray.__version__)"
python -c "import cartopy; print(cartopy.__version__)"
python -c "import matplotlib; print(matplotlib.__version__)"
```

`{note} This course was written for CDO version 2.x. Most commands are also compatible with CDO 1.9.x, but some newer operators may not be available. Check the CDO changelog for version-specific features.`

